    {
      "id": 17592186048963,
      "mathAcademyId": 1423,
      "title": "Solving Perfect Square Quadratic Equations",
      "uuid": "cfc9b8f9-d7e2-5dfb-84a8-1f6a04303420"
    }
  ],
  "sections": [
    {
      "contentId": 17592186111919,
      "id": "5f5183e9-19ad-5c69-807b-510db46d8281",
      "kind": "tutorial",
      "knowledgePoint": null,
      "markdown": "Many quadratic equations have two solutions. However, it's also possible for a quadratic equation to have only one solution or no real solutions at all.\n\nFor example, consider the following quadratic equation:\n\n$$\n(3x-6)^2=0\n$$\n\nWe can solve this equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3x-6)^{2} & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{(3x-6)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{0} \\\\ |3x-6| & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNormally, at this stage, we would obtain two solutions by setting the inside of the absolute value equal to positive or negative $(\\pm)$ the right-hand side. However, in this case, the right-hand side is zero, and $+0$ and $-0$ are both just $0.$ \n\nSo, we have only one solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|3x-6| & =0 \\\\ 3x-6 & =0 \\\\ 3x & =6 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "mathAcademyId": "9203",
      "stepId": "17592186111517",
      "title": "Introduction"
    },
    {
      "contentId": 17592186110346,
      "id": "6967ba33-4930-52a7-a7b4-7ffe0762be89",
      "kind": "example",
      "knowledgePoint": {
        "id": 17592186110612,
        "title": "Cases When the Perfect Square is Isolated"
      },
      "markdown": "Solve $-2(3k-12)^2 = 0.$",
      "mathAcademyId": "e-14817",
      "stepId": "17592186111518",
      "title": "Cases When the Perfect Square is Isolated",
      "workedSolution": "We can solve this equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2(3k-12)^{2} & =0 \\\\ (3k-12)^{2} & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{(3k-12)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{0} \\\\ |3k-12| & =0 \\\\ 3k-12 & =0 \\\\ 3k & =12 \\\\ k & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$"
    },
    {
      "contentId": 17592186110348,
      "id": "bedc7efe-42c1-54d6-8c86-490434760721",
      "kind": "example",
      "knowledgePoint": {
        "id": 17592186110613,
        "title": "Finding an Unknown Parameter"
      },
      "markdown": "Which value of $k$ gives precisely one solution to the equation $(x+3)^2+10=k?$",
      "mathAcademyId": "e-14820",
      "stepId": "17592186111519",
      "title": "Finding an Unknown Parameter",
      "workedSolution": "First, we solve for $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)^{2}+10 & =k \\\\ (x+3)^{2} & =k-10 \\\\ \\sqrt{(x+3)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{k-10} \\\\ |x+3| & =\\sqrt{k-10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation has only one solution if the right-hand side is equal to zero. So, we solve for the value of $k$ that makes the right-hand side equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{k-10} & =0 \\\\ k-10 & =0 \\\\ k & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=10$ will give only one solution to the equation."
    },
    {
      "contentId": 17592186110349,
      "id": "8ba31f89-a15d-5135-be97-97d5978bcacc",
      "kind": "example",
      "knowledgePoint": {
        "id": 17592186110614,
        "title": "Factoring a Perfect Square"
      },
      "markdown": "Given that $g^2 -18g + 81 = 0$, what are the values of $g?$",
      "mathAcademyId": "e-14822",
      "stepId": "17592186111520",
      "title": "Factoring a Perfect Square",
      "workedSolution": "First, notice that the left-hand side is a perfect square. It just needs to be factored:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{2}-18g+81 & =0 \\\\ {g}^{2}-2\\cdot 9\\cdot g+{9}^{2} & =0 \\\\ (g-9)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{(g-9)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{0} \\\\ |g-9| & =0 \\\\ g-9 & =0 \\\\ g & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$"
    },
    {
      "contentId": 17592186111920,
      "id": "e56ff91b-5edd-5f4c-9d04-0fdf7c34e322",
      "kind": "tutorial",
      "knowledgePoint": null,
      "markdown": "Let's try and solve the equation $(x+1)^2=-1$ using the square root method.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2} & =-1 \\\\ \\sqrt{(x+1)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{-1} \\\\ |x+1| & =\\sqrt{-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe have a problem here. We cannot take the square root of a negative number. For that reason, the equation above has *no real solutions*.",
      "mathAcademyId": "9205",
      "stepId": "17592186111521",
      "title": "Perfect Square Quadratic Equations With No Real Solutions"
    },
    {
      "contentId": 17592186110350,
      "id": "c852bd3c-c398-5a0c-9830-c6b1283797c0",
      "kind": "example",
      "knowledgePoint": {
        "id": 17592186110615,
        "title": "Equations With No Real Solutions"
      },
      "markdown": "Consider the equation $(4x+1)^2 +2=3k.$ For which values of $k$ does this equation have no real solutions?",
      "mathAcademyId": "e-14824",
      "stepId": "17592186111522",
      "title": "Equations With No Real Solutions",
      "workedSolution": "Let's try and solve using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4x+1)^{2}+2 & =3k \\\\ (4x+1)^{2} & =3k-2 \\\\ \\sqrt{(4x+1)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{3k-2} \\\\ |4x+1| & =\\sqrt{3k-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation will have no real solutions if the expression under the square root is negative, which gives the following condition:\n\n$$\n3k-2 < 0\n$$\n\nWe solve this inequality for $k\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3k-2 & <0 \\\\ 3k & <2 \\\\ k & <\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, any value of $k$ that is less than $\\dfrac 2 3$ will give an equation with no real solutions."
    }
  ],
